  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Stalled front remains to the south of \nENC this afternoon with ongoing shower and thunderstorm activity \ndeveloping along it across the coastal waters. Seabreeze is \ncurrently moving inland this afternoon as well and have kept the\nSChc PoPs in the forecast along the NE'rn zones where best\nconvergence is located. While the threat for a pop up shower or\nstorm is low it is non-zero. While the front has remained to \nthe south today, flow has begun to shift and become more onshore\nwith SE'rly breeze noted across ENC this afternoon allowing for\nincreased moisture to begin to advect into the area from S-N. \nHighs have gotten into the 80s to near 90 in places as well, \nresulting in a slightly more humid day today as compared to \nyesterday. The aforementioned front is forecast to begin to lift\nnorth later this evening as a wave of low pressure develops in \nthe SE and rides NE'wards along the front resulting in \nincreasing chances for precip lifting northwards tonight across \nENC. Greatest chances to see shower and thunderstorm activity \nwould be east of Hwy 17 and along the OBX. With cloudcover \nforecast to overspread ENC tonight, lows get into the low 70s. \n \nKEY MESSAGE 2... The aforementioned low pressure system is \nforecast to lift NE'wards across the area along the stalled \nfront in the early morning hours THU, pushing the boundary north\ntoward, and perhaps over, the ENC coast, providing a better \nfocal point for greater coverage of showers and storms ahead of\na second front which will cross the area by Sat. Out of all the\ndays, Thurs looks to have the least amount of activity with \nshowers and thunderstorms being more isolated to widely \nscattered in nature, though this will still be an increase in \nprecip chances as compared to previous days with PoPs generally \naround 30-50% through much of the day Thurs. Activity will \nlikely be ongoing along the OBX and far eastern zones Thurs \nmorning and then push inland as the day goes on. Biggest \nquestion marks for tomorrow will be how focused activity will be\nand how much instability can build through the day. PWATs do \ncreep back north of 1.75\" and likely get close to 2\" as we \napproach the evening hours Thurs, but cloud cover may cap MLCAPE\nvalues around 1000-2000 J/kg max and with little to no wind \nshear in place, any shower or tstm that does develop is not \nanticipated to be very organized. The aforementioned front \nremains around the area Thurs night into Fri promoting even \ngreater chances for more widespread shower and thunderstorm \nactivity as moisture and instability continues to advect into \nthe region. \n\nWith enough instability in place, and subtly increasing amounts of \nshear as trough aloft over Canada digs toward the Great Lakes \nFri, a few isolated stronger storms aren't completely out of the\nquestion. In addition to this, any shower or thunderstorm that \ndevelops will be an efficient rainmaker, potentially bringing \ntorrential rainfall and a risk for flash flooding, mainly in \nurban and poor drainage areas. As such, SPC has a Marginal Risk \n(lvl 1/5) of severe tstorms and WPC has a Slight Risk (lvl 2/4)\nof excessive rainfall for Fri while the FA is warm sectored \nwith the front that has been offshore then being stalled along \nor near the AppMts. Rain continues into the weekend when a front\npushed by the digging trough from the Great Lakes will cross \nthe FA on Sat. Temps through the weekend remain seasonable with \nhighs in the 80s and lows in the upper 60s to mid 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Behind the departing cold front, upper level and \nsurface high pressure builds back over the area Sun into early next \nweek. This will bring a return to more climatological precip chances \nalong the seabreeze as well as rising temperatures, low to mid \n90s/mid 70s splits and MaxAppTs in excess of 100deg from TUES onward \nnext week.",
